# Line endings are decided here, not by each clone's core.autocrlf.
#
# Why: the pipeline parses these files (Gitea reads the workflow YAML,
# ci/tests/verify-workflow.check.mjs matches multi-line anchors, pwsh runs the
# scripts). With core.autocrlf=true and no policy here, the same committed bytes
# can check out as LF on one machine and CRLF on another, which makes a
# content-neutral checkout look like a real difference.
#
# Every pattern below is content-neutral for this repository: the committed blobs
# of these types are already LF-only, so pinning the working-tree style changes no
# file content - it only stops git from rewriting them at checkout.
#
# LabVIEW payloads (.vi, .lvclass, .ctl, .lvproj, .lvlib) are deliberately NOT
# listed: their stored line endings are already inconsistent between files, so any
# renormalisation here would rewrite project content. Leave them to LabVIEW.

*.yml    text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.mjs    text eol=lf
*.js     text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.txt    text eol=lf
*.toml   text eol=lf
*.ini    text eol=lf
*.ps1    text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf

# Batch files are pinned to LF, not CRLF: the one .cmd this pipeline runs
# (ci/tests/fixtures/LabVIEWCLI-stub.cmd) is LF in the working tree today and the
# suites pass against it, so this records the proven state instead of guessing at
# a cmd.exe quirk that has not shown up here.
*.cmd    text eol=lf
